1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,285
Here's a question for a cheese expert:

2
00:00:02,285 --> 00:00:06,315
describe where cheese making is important
in the instructor's book.

3
00:00:06,635 --> 00:00:10,992
In the staff's recorded run,
these two lines print before the answer:

4
00:00:10,992 --> 00:00:14,528
a function called get_book_by_author,
and its arguments.

5
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:19,253
Ten passages came back,
all from one book, filtered by author.

6
00:00:19,573 --> 00:00:24,288
But Gemini runs in Google's data center,
where localhost means nothing.

7
00:00:24,608 --> 00:00:27,509
So who searched your database,
and who chose the book?

8
00:00:27,750 --> 00:00:30,026
The lecture asks a bigger version:

9
00:00:30,026 --> 00:00:34,577
plan five days in Paris,
and replan if it rains or a flight is late.

10
00:00:34,897 --> 00:00:36,489
A model can draft a plan,

11
00:00:36,489 --> 00:00:39,355
and RAG can fetch facts,
but neither can act.

12
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,027
What's missing:
live systems, tools that act,

13
00:00:43,027 --> 00:00:44,843
and the autonomy to decide.

14
00:00:45,083 --> 00:00:47,643
Today we climb toward that in three steps.

15
00:00:47,963 --> 00:00:51,354
By hand, with llm-rag's agent;
with a framework,

16
00:00:51,354 --> 00:00:56,369
Google's Agent Development Kit;
and with a harness, in Colab notebooks.

17
00:00:57,417 --> 00:00:59,821
The tracker, top left, shows the step;

18
00:00:59,821 --> 00:01:02,985
the gray tag,
top right, names the lecture slides.

19
00:01:03,305 --> 00:01:06,654
Pause at each yellow your-turn tag,
and run the command.

20
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:18,055
You'll need the database
and key from lecture 5,

21
00:01:18,055 --> 00:01:20,796
Docker, uv, Node, and two more keys:

22
00:01:20,796 --> 00:01:25,365
one from Google AI Studio,
one from OpenAI, with billing on.

23
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:31,037
We recorded on October 18, 2026,
without keys.

24
00:01:31,357 --> 00:01:37,501
Docker, Chroma, uv, the ADK web server
and two MCP servers ran for real;

25
00:01:37,501 --> 00:01:41,597
every model call stopped unsent,
or was refused.

26
00:01:41,917 --> 00:01:44,563
Model answers are the staff's saved outputs.

27
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:48,448
On screen we hide the instructor's name;
your terminal shows it.

28
00:01:48,768 --> 00:01:53,056
Every command is in the study guide,
in the MLOps_Learning repo.

29
00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:57,361
First the ideas, in the Cheese Quiz.

30
00:01:57,681 --> 00:01:59,205
Save a copy in Drive,

31
00:01:59,205 --> 00:02:02,542
then add your OpenAI key
in the Secrets panel,

32
00:02:02,542 --> 00:02:05,831
under the name shown,
with notebook access on.

33
00:02:06,067 --> 00:02:09,579
The install cell installs two packages,
unpinned,

34
00:02:09,579 --> 00:02:11,084
and hides all output:

35
00:02:11,084 --> 00:02:14,899
if imports fail later,
rerun it without that redirect.

36
00:02:15,219 --> 00:02:18,568
Then download the quiz files,
and pause to run the setup.

37
00:02:20,817 --> 00:02:23,647
Now the notebook
asks one question three ways,

38
00:02:23,647 --> 00:02:25,492
with the same model each time:

39
00:02:25,492 --> 00:02:28,710
what's the best cheese
for a grilled cheese sandwich?

40
00:02:29,030 --> 00:02:35,686
Lane 1, the base LLM: one call to gpt-4o,
one answer, in under 3 seconds.

41
00:02:35,933 --> 00:02:40,498
Lane 2, the reasoning LLM:
Python methods run in a fixed order.

42
00:02:40,818 --> 00:02:46,280
Analyze, split into 4 sub-questions,
answer and verify each, then combine.

43
00:02:46,600 --> 00:02:51,763
That's 11 calls and 39 seconds,
and its token total is only an estimate.

44
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,267
The lecture calls a multi-call system
like this a reasoning engine.

45
00:02:56,587 --> 00:02:58,586
Here your Python orders the calls,

46
00:02:58,586 --> 00:03:01,643
so it's a reasoning workflow,
not a reasoning model.

47
00:03:02,883 --> 00:03:05,485
Lane 3, the agent: the same model,

48
00:03:05,485 --> 00:03:09,923
plus instructions
and a web search tool that OpenAI hosts.

49
00:03:10,243 --> 00:03:15,320
Its answer reports two searches,
and it stopped on its own after 13 seconds.

50
00:03:15,640 --> 00:03:20,761
It also reports one tool used,
but that counts tools configured, not calls.

51
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,989
The lecture defines an agent
as an LLM, given instructions and tools,

52
00:03:25,989 --> 00:03:29,171
that runs on its own until the task is done.

53
00:03:29,491 --> 00:03:32,471
Of its five blocks,
one decides the next step:

54
00:03:32,471 --> 00:03:34,803
dynamic control, the steering wheel.

55
00:03:35,050 --> 00:03:39,935
So an agent isn't a smarter model:
the model was the same in all three lanes.

56
00:03:40,255 --> 00:03:42,687
What changed is who steers, and the tools.

57
00:03:43,007 --> 00:03:46,271
Every call is paid;
pause, and run the three lanes.

58
00:03:49,317 --> 00:03:52,112
Next section: managing the model's inputs.

59
00:03:52,432 --> 00:03:54,522
LLMs are stateless functions.

60
00:03:54,842 --> 00:03:59,088
Every call is a fresh envelope,
and the model knows only what's inside.

61
00:03:59,334 --> 00:04:02,204
The notebook keeps
the model and question fixed,

62
00:04:02,204 --> 00:04:04,177
and changes the inputs each step.

63
00:04:04,497 --> 00:04:08,006
A system prompt,
retrieved documents, earlier turns,

64
00:04:08,006 --> 00:04:12,326
a profile from past sessions,
a tool's output, an output schema.

65
00:04:12,567 --> 00:04:14,314
Watch the top pick change:

66
00:04:14,314 --> 00:04:18,348
cheddar, American,
then mild cheeses for kids, then Havarti.

67
00:04:18,668 --> 00:04:23,788
Then a mild cheddar from Whole Foods,
and finally the same pick as strict JSON.

68
00:04:24,034 --> 00:04:26,295
Step 4 shows what memory really is.

69
00:04:26,615 --> 00:04:30,989
The earlier turns aren't remembered:
the code sends them again, every time.

70
00:04:31,309 --> 00:04:34,018
Memory is paper you put back
in the envelope.

71
00:04:34,267 --> 00:04:37,765
The lecture groups these sheets
into two building blocks:

72
00:04:37,765 --> 00:04:39,238
instructions and memory.

73
00:04:39,558 --> 00:04:42,928
Next lecture names the craft:
context engineering.

74
00:04:43,167 --> 00:04:45,535
Now look closely at the code of step 2.

75
00:04:45,855 --> 00:04:47,242
Can you spot the slip?

76
00:04:51,484 --> 00:04:54,051
The system prompt goes
in with the role user,

77
00:04:54,051 --> 00:04:56,220
and the question with the role system.

78
00:04:56,467 --> 00:05:00,755
Swap those two roles before comparing;
from step 3 on, they're right.

79
00:05:01,075 --> 00:05:03,059
Pause, and run the seven steps.

80
00:05:05,300 --> 00:05:06,525
And notice step 6:

81
00:05:06,525 --> 00:05:09,929
the code pasted a tool's output
into the envelope.

82
00:05:10,249 --> 00:05:12,212
But how does a model ask for a tool?

83
00:05:13,850 --> 00:05:19,226
In lecture 5, RAG was a straight line:
question, retrieval, model, response.

84
00:05:19,546 --> 00:05:23,750
The lecture's recap makes retrieval smarter:
steps around the search,

85
00:05:23,750 --> 00:05:27,589
whether to retrieve at all,
grading, rewriting, and web search.

86
00:05:27,833 --> 00:05:30,811
Agentic RAG puts a model
in front of that menu,

87
00:05:30,811 --> 00:05:32,015
and lets it choose.

88
00:05:32,250 --> 00:05:37,029
llm-rag's Agents section
is Agentic RAG with a menu of two.

89
00:05:37,349 --> 00:05:40,997
Its README says:
let the LLM decide how to retrieve.

90
00:05:41,317 --> 00:05:44,005
Search one author's book,
or search all books.

91
00:05:44,250 --> 00:05:47,125
Here's the first menu item, written by hand:

92
00:05:47,125 --> 00:05:50,981
a name, which the model will write back,
and a description;

93
00:05:50,981 --> 00:05:55,621
with the parameter descriptions,
the only documentation the model sees.

94
00:05:55,941 --> 00:05:58,245
Parameters come as a JSON schema.

95
00:05:58,483 --> 00:06:01,363
The author must be one of 9 names
in an enum.

96
00:06:01,683 --> 00:06:05,075
The search text's description
even coaches the model:

97
00:06:05,075 --> 00:06:07,891
expand the search term to a sentence or two.

98
00:06:08,211 --> 00:06:09,918
And both fields are required.

99
00:06:10,167 --> 00:06:11,660
Now the real function.

100
00:06:11,980 --> 00:06:15,580
Two more arguments,
the collection and an embedding function,

101
00:06:15,580 --> 00:06:18,295
aren't in the schema:
your code supplies them.

102
00:06:18,615 --> 00:06:21,857
The model controls only the author
and the search text.

103
00:06:24,200 --> 00:06:28,042
The lecture says
a tool-using agent must pick the right tool,

104
00:06:28,042 --> 00:06:30,877
format its inputs,
and interpret its outputs.

105
00:06:31,197 --> 00:06:34,405
Here the model picks
and formats only by writing;

106
00:06:34,405 --> 00:06:35,976
your code runs the tool.

107
00:06:36,217 --> 00:06:40,803
Here are the 11 books in the collection,
beside the 9 names in the enum.

108
00:06:50,650 --> 00:06:51,650
Two books.

109
00:06:51,909 --> 00:06:57,861
The cottage cheese recipe book, 178 chunks,
has an author who isn't on the list.

110
00:06:58,181 --> 00:07:03,759
The Woman's Institute book,
1,505 chunks, has no author at all:

111
00:07:03,759 --> 00:07:06,415
a file name slip in lecture 5.

112
00:07:06,650 --> 00:07:09,807
Both stay reachable
by the search across all books.

113
00:07:10,127 --> 00:07:12,805
Now the stage: Gemini in Google's cloud,

114
00:07:12,805 --> 00:07:16,421
two containers on your laptop,
and a boundary between.

115
00:07:16,667 --> 00:07:19,718
The model never gets your database,
only a menu.

116
00:07:20,038 --> 00:07:23,643
Call 1 returns an order:
a function name and arguments.

117
00:07:23,963 --> 00:07:27,163
Your code runs it;
call 2 sends back the result.

118
00:07:32,750 --> 00:07:36,334
Lecture 5 ended on one command;
today we run it.

119
00:07:36,654 --> 00:07:37,806
Starting fresh?

120
00:07:38,126 --> 00:07:41,378
Clone llm-rag,
and put the service account key

121
00:07:41,378 --> 00:07:43,500
in a secrets folder beside it;

122
00:07:43,500 --> 00:07:45,550
otherwise, go to your folder.

123
00:07:45,783 --> 00:07:48,130
Step 1: cd llm-rag.

124
00:07:48,450 --> 00:07:54,548
Step 2: edit docker-shell.sh,
and set GCP_PROJECT to your own project ID;

125
00:07:54,548 --> 00:07:56,386
we used a placeholder.

126
00:07:56,706 --> 00:07:58,029
Pause, and do both.

127
00:08:00,267 --> 00:08:03,062
Step 3: sh docker-shell.sh.

128
00:08:03,382 --> 00:08:05,152
Compose starts Chroma first.

129
00:08:05,472 --> 00:08:08,408
The pull message
after it looks like an error

130
00:08:08,408 --> 00:08:09,452
but is harmless:

131
00:08:09,452 --> 00:08:12,725
there's no such image online,
so Compose builds it.

132
00:08:13,033 --> 00:08:16,190
After about 40 seconds of building,
the banner prints.

133
00:08:16,510 --> 00:08:23,081
Python 3.12.15 and uv 0.13.1,
at the time of recording.

134
00:08:23,401 --> 00:08:27,310
After the at sign comes the container's ID,
new every run;

135
00:08:27,310 --> 00:08:29,332
pause, and get to this prompt.

136
00:08:31,567 --> 00:08:35,023
An extra check from a second terminal:
docker ps.

137
00:08:35,343 --> 00:08:39,673
Chroma publishes port 8000;
remember that port for part 2.

138
00:08:40,600 --> 00:08:43,529
Now the Agents section, step 1:

139
00:08:43,529 --> 00:08:47,875
python cli.py --agent
--chunk_type char-split.

140
00:08:48,195 --> 00:08:52,120
On a fresh clone it fails at once:
the collection does not exist.

141
00:08:52,440 --> 00:08:56,301
That's line 429, before any call to Gemini.

142
00:08:56,550 --> 00:08:59,387
The agent needs the index from lecture 5.

143
00:08:59,707 --> 00:09:01,094
Kept that database?

144
00:09:01,414 --> 00:09:03,687
It's in the docker-volumes/ folder;

145
00:09:03,687 --> 00:09:07,323
if you chunked before September 22,
pull and rebuild it.

146
00:09:07,643 --> 00:09:10,182
Otherwise, chunk, embed and load again.

147
00:09:10,417 --> 00:09:15,025
We had no key, so the staff's committed
embeddings stood in for the embed step.

148
00:09:15,267 --> 00:09:16,971
Then the load step:

149
00:09:16,971 --> 00:09:21,006
python cli.py --load
--chunk_type char-split.

150
00:09:21,326 --> 00:09:27,982
11 books, 8,248 chunks;
the instructor's story is just 24 of them.

151
00:09:28,302 --> 00:09:31,821
An extra --get command
confirms the collection answers;

152
00:09:31,821 --> 00:09:33,229
pause, and load yours.

153
00:09:36,650 --> 00:09:41,107
After the collection lookup,
the agent function builds the user's turn:

154
00:09:41,107 --> 00:09:43,178
the question, with the role user.

155
00:09:43,498 --> 00:09:45,781
Then comes the first call to Gemini.

156
00:09:46,017 --> 00:09:49,384
Three settings:
temperature zero, the menu as tools,

157
00:09:49,384 --> 00:09:51,585
and a tool_config with mode="any".

158
00:09:51,905 --> 00:09:54,785
The lecture lists temperature
as a design choice.

159
00:09:55,105 --> 00:09:57,750
Zero keeps the tool
choice nearly repeatable.

160
00:09:57,983 --> 00:10:02,356
An extra one-liner shows
what mode="any" becomes: the enum value ANY.

161
00:10:02,676 --> 00:10:04,959
The model must answer with a function call.

162
00:10:05,200 --> 00:10:09,199
In the lecture, a good agent
may decide not to search at all;

163
00:10:09,199 --> 00:10:11,493
Self-RAG starts with that decision.

164
00:10:11,813 --> 00:10:15,291
Ours can't: on call 1,
mode="any" forces a tool call.

165
00:10:15,611 --> 00:10:17,723
It chooses which search, never whether.

166
00:10:17,967 --> 00:10:20,143
What does Gemini actually receive?

167
00:10:20,463 --> 00:10:22,619
An extra script runs the same code,

168
00:10:22,619 --> 00:10:25,391
but prints the request instead
of sending it.

169
00:10:25,711 --> 00:10:29,453
One envelope: the question,
both declarations with the enum;

170
00:10:29,453 --> 00:10:31,449
"mode": "ANY", temperature zero.

171
00:10:31,683 --> 00:10:38,510
And no system instruction: as in lecture 5,
cli.py defines one and never sends it.

172
00:10:38,750 --> 00:10:41,459
Now step 1 again,
with the collection loaded.

173
00:10:41,779 --> 00:10:47,134
The lookup passes and the question prints,
but the first model call needs your key:

174
00:10:47,134 --> 00:10:49,715
DefaultCredentialsError, file not found.

175
00:10:49,950 --> 00:10:51,422
Here's what the staff saw.

176
00:10:51,742 --> 00:10:54,596
The model's whole answer is a function call:

177
00:10:54,596 --> 00:10:57,839
get_book_by_author,
with the instructor as author,

178
00:10:57,839 --> 00:11:01,406
and the search text,
cheese making importance location.

179
00:11:01,650 --> 00:11:05,042
Four words, though the description
asked for a sentence or two.

180
00:11:05,362 --> 00:11:09,159
There's also an ID, and a thought signature;
we'll meet it again.

181
00:11:09,400 --> 00:11:12,899
The SDK ran nothing;
it only has declarations.

182
00:11:13,219 --> 00:11:15,032
So far, one paid call.

183
00:11:15,352 --> 00:11:17,827
Pause here, and run step 1 with your key.

184
00:11:21,667 --> 00:11:25,418
Back in your Python,
a dispatcher matches each call's name,

185
00:11:25,418 --> 00:11:28,597
runs the real function
with the model's arguments,

186
00:11:28,597 --> 00:11:31,395
and wraps the result as a function response.

187
00:11:31,634 --> 00:11:35,431
Without a key, an extra script
feeds the staff's recorded call

188
00:11:35,431 --> 00:11:39,228
through the tutorial's own dispatcher,
against our collection.

189
00:11:39,467 --> 00:11:43,351
One stand-in: without Google
we can't embed the search text,

190
00:11:43,351 --> 00:11:45,746
so a stored chunk's vector stands in,

191
00:11:45,746 --> 00:11:48,918
and the 10 chunks may differ
from a real query's.

192
00:11:49,167 --> 00:11:53,263
Out come 10 passages from today's story,
about Mara and Anja.

193
00:11:53,583 --> 00:12:00,388
Joined into one string of 3,509 characters,
they're stapled to the call as its response.

194
00:12:00,634 --> 00:12:04,837
Call 2 sends three things:
the question, the model's own turn,

195
00:12:04,837 --> 00:12:07,482
unchanged, and your function responses.

196
00:12:07,802 --> 00:12:10,745
The tools go along again,
but no tool_config:

197
00:12:10,745 --> 00:12:12,708
the mode is back to automatic.

198
00:12:12,950 --> 00:12:16,506
Inside, the model's turn
carries its thought signature:

199
00:12:16,506 --> 00:12:21,355
Gemini's thinking models sign their turn,
and want it back exactly as sent.

200
00:12:21,675 --> 00:12:24,406
Resending the turn unchanged keeps the seal.

201
00:12:24,650 --> 00:12:25,887
The staff's answer?

202
00:12:26,207 --> 00:12:29,248
An Alpine valley,
its caves, and transhumance,

203
00:12:29,248 --> 00:12:31,562
the seasonal movement of livestock.

204
00:12:31,882 --> 00:12:37,493
The two calls used 247 and 1,269 tokens.

205
00:12:37,734 --> 00:12:39,590
So, who searched your database?

206
00:12:39,910 --> 00:12:44,795
Gemini wrote the call and chose the book;
your Python ran it, on your laptop.

207
00:12:45,115 --> 00:12:46,481
That's two model calls

208
00:12:46,481 --> 00:12:49,339
and one embedding call per question,
all paid.

209
00:12:49,584 --> 00:12:54,551
But today's book never says transhumance,
nor three other words from this run:

210
00:12:54,551 --> 00:12:58,117
the commit that added this output
also rewrote the book.

211
00:12:58,437 --> 00:13:01,061
The staff's index
was built from the old text.

212
00:13:01,300 --> 00:13:04,884
Your run on today's book draws
on passages like these:

213
00:13:04,884 --> 00:13:06,676
Mara, the curd, the wheels.

214
00:13:06,996 --> 00:13:09,215
An index answers from what was loaded.

215
00:13:09,450 --> 00:13:13,488
One small slip:
the README promises the response,

216
00:13:13,488 --> 00:13:17,279
but line 478 prints
the whole response object.

217
00:13:17,599 --> 00:13:20,245
Print response.text for just the answer.

218
00:13:20,484 --> 00:13:24,031
Step 2 links to the course's hosted
Cheese Agent Tool,

219
00:13:24,031 --> 00:13:27,054
up at the time of recording;
try it in Chrome.

220
00:13:29,400 --> 00:13:32,472
The lecture's agent runs
until the task is done.

221
00:13:32,792 --> 00:13:37,443
llm-rag's agent ran one lap:
a forced tool call, then an answer.

222
00:13:37,763 --> 00:13:39,612
The README calls it an agent;

223
00:13:39,612 --> 00:13:43,437
by the lecture's definition,
it's the smallest piece of one.

224
00:13:43,683 --> 00:13:45,902
Here's a question about that one lap.

225
00:13:54,750 --> 00:13:58,295
It prints a response
whose only part is a function call,

226
00:13:58,295 --> 00:14:00,510
with no text, and the program ends.

227
00:14:00,830 --> 00:14:04,414
Nothing runs that call;
a loop would run it, and ask again.

228
00:14:04,650 --> 00:14:07,106
The lecture draws the agent as a hub:

229
00:14:07,106 --> 00:14:10,690
the core and its instructions
become a decision maker,

230
00:14:10,690 --> 00:14:13,610
with spokes to a planner, tools, and memory.

231
00:14:13,930 --> 00:14:16,362
Unroll the spoke to the tools into a loop.

232
00:14:16,600 --> 00:14:17,837
What ends the loop?

233
00:14:18,157 --> 00:14:22,893
The stop rule: a reply
with no function call is the final response.

234
00:14:23,213 --> 00:14:26,733
The model decides it's done
by answering in plain text.

235
00:14:26,983 --> 00:14:31,592
In code you see the loop from outside:
the ADK Runner streams events,

236
00:14:31,592 --> 00:14:36,669
this wrapper keeps the final response,
and the loop runs inside the library.

237
00:14:36,917 --> 00:14:39,764
You also cap it, in case it never stops:

238
00:14:39,764 --> 00:14:44,533
12 turns in the Cheese Quiz Guild,
16 iterations in the newsletter.

239
00:14:44,853 --> 00:14:48,522
Hitting the Agents SDK cap
ends the run with an error.

240
00:14:48,767 --> 00:14:52,074
The lecture's building blocks
gray out dynamic control.

241
00:14:52,394 --> 00:14:55,977
You configure the model,
instructions, tools and memory;

242
00:14:55,977 --> 00:14:59,113
the loop is the block
a framework writes for you.

243
00:14:59,350 --> 00:15:01,227
Before part 2, tear down.

244
00:15:01,547 --> 00:15:07,350
Type exit; an extra docker ps
then shows Chroma still up, on port 8000.

245
00:15:07,670 --> 00:15:11,553
Then docker compose down:
two harmless warnings, and it stops.

246
00:15:11,867 --> 00:15:16,368
Your index stays on disk,
and part 2 also needs port 8000.

247
00:15:16,688 --> 00:15:18,011
Pause and tear down.

248
00:15:18,331 --> 00:15:21,253
In part 2,
a framework runs this loop for us.

249
00:15:24,300 --> 00:15:26,412
Part 2: frameworks own the loop.

250
00:15:26,732 --> 00:15:28,195
No Docker this time:

251
00:15:28,195 --> 00:15:33,388
Google's Agent Development Kit runs
in a uv environment on your laptop.

252
00:15:33,708 --> 00:15:36,353
Open a new terminal
where you keep your clones.

253
00:15:36,600 --> 00:15:40,760
Step 1: clone; the README writes
a placeholder for the address.

254
00:15:41,080 --> 00:15:46,221
The next line says cd google-adk-tutorial,
but no such folder exists.

255
00:15:46,467 --> 00:15:49,774
A small slip:
the folder is named after the repo.

256
00:15:50,094 --> 00:15:53,443
Type cd llm-agent-google-adk instead.

257
00:15:53,683 --> 00:15:56,995
An extra ls
shows six numbered module folders,

258
00:15:56,995 --> 00:15:58,219
but no lock file:

259
00:15:58,219 --> 00:16:02,259
the repo ignores it,
so everyone resolves fresh versions.

260
00:16:02,500 --> 00:16:05,743
Next, copy .env.example to .env.

261
00:16:06,063 --> 00:16:10,231
An extra grep shows
a placeholder Google API key,

262
00:16:10,231 --> 00:16:12,953
and the model, gemini-2.5-flash.

263
00:16:13,200 --> 00:16:16,423
Then create a key in Google AI Studio,

264
00:16:16,423 --> 00:16:20,325
and paste it into .env,
after GOOGLE_API_KEY=.

265
00:16:20,567 --> 00:16:22,338
Also change the model line.

266
00:16:22,658 --> 00:16:27,268
Choosing the LLM core
is the lecture's first design decision,

267
00:16:27,268 --> 00:16:32,407
and here it's one line:
a current model, like gemini-3.1-flash-lite.

268
00:16:32,650 --> 00:16:36,676
Google lists the code's fallback,
gemini-2.0-flash,

269
00:16:36,676 --> 00:16:41,333
as shut down, and limits
gemini-2.5-flash to earlier users.

270
00:16:41,653 --> 00:16:45,898
Keep everything in this root file:
only the nearest .env is loaded.

271
00:16:46,218 --> 00:16:47,861
Pause, and set up yours.

272
00:16:50,100 --> 00:16:57,268
The project file wants Python 3.10 or newer
and ADK 1.8 or newer, with no upper bound.

273
00:16:57,588 --> 00:17:00,553
It also lists
Chroma and sentence-transformers.

274
00:17:00,800 --> 00:17:04,085
One more fact:
this laptop has no Python at all.

275
00:17:10,933 --> 00:17:12,853
Then install: uv sync.

276
00:17:13,173 --> 00:17:18,985
As in lecture 1, with no Python around,
uv downloads the newest:

277
00:17:18,985 --> 00:17:22,709
CPython 3.15.0, at the time of recording.

278
00:17:22,950 --> 00:17:26,491
It resolves 189 packages, then fails.

279
00:17:26,811 --> 00:17:32,977
onnxruntime, which Chroma pulls in,
has wheels only up to Python 3.14.

280
00:17:33,217 --> 00:17:38,335
So this step fails whenever uv picks 3.15:
on a fresh laptop,

281
00:17:38,335 --> 00:17:43,201
or where uv already has 3.15,
even beside an older Python.

282
00:17:43,521 --> 00:17:48,129
The fix, with an extra command:
uv python pin 3.13.

283
00:17:49,567 --> 00:17:58,122
Then uv sync again: CPython 3.13 this time,
and it installs, ADK 2.12.0 included.

284
00:17:58,442 --> 00:18:01,965
PyTorch makes it big: about 1.6 gigabytes,

285
00:18:01,965 --> 00:18:05,823
plus about 3 gigabytes
for CUDA on a Linux PC.

286
00:18:06,067 --> 00:18:11,116
An extra check,
uv run adk --version, says 2.12.0,

287
00:18:11,116 --> 00:18:13,640
at the time of recording.

288
00:18:13,960 --> 00:18:16,093
Pause, and get your sync to succeed.

289
00:18:19,433 --> 00:18:25,001
Running Agents, step 1,
from the repository root: uv run adk web.

290
00:18:25,321 --> 00:18:30,235
First, version 2.12 asks a question
the README doesn't mention:

291
00:18:30,235 --> 00:18:32,574
may Google collect usage data?

292
00:18:32,816 --> 00:18:35,697
The text says telemetry is off by default,

293
00:18:35,697 --> 00:18:39,195
but the capital Y means
pressing enter answers yes.

294
00:18:39,515 --> 00:18:41,013
Answer as you like;

295
00:18:41,013 --> 00:18:44,955
change it later with uv run
adk telemetry disable.

296
00:18:45,200 --> 00:18:47,973
Then the server starts, on port 8000.

297
00:18:48,293 --> 00:18:51,800
Step 2: open localhost:8000 in your browser;

298
00:18:51,800 --> 00:18:55,546
an extra curl shows the redirect
to the dev UI.

299
00:18:55,783 --> 00:18:59,964
The agent dropdown lists 7 entries,
nested ones included.

300
00:19:00,284 --> 00:19:04,766
Pick 1-baseline, as its README says,
and the page refuses:

301
00:19:04,766 --> 00:19:08,242
agent names
must be valid Python identifiers.

302
00:19:08,483 --> 00:19:11,810
Why?
Each folder name becomes the app's name,

303
00:19:11,810 --> 00:19:14,546
and an identifier holds only letters,

304
00:19:14,546 --> 00:19:17,891
digits and underscores,
never starting with a digit.

305
00:19:18,211 --> 00:19:20,707
The name 1-baseline breaks both rules.

306
00:19:20,950 --> 00:19:26,155
An older version, 1.27.5, lists
only the four folders.

307
00:19:26,475 --> 00:19:29,697
But picking one still fails,
as an invalid app name.

308
00:19:30,017 --> 00:19:32,896
So a version pin doesn't help;
we undid ours.

309
00:19:34,416 --> 00:19:37,744
What works is renaming,
which isn't in the README either.

310
00:19:38,064 --> 00:19:42,125
Stop the server with Ctrl+C,
then drop each leading number,

311
00:19:42,125 --> 00:19:44,464
and turn hyphens into underscores.

312
00:19:44,700 --> 00:19:47,431
Start the web server again,
and pick baseline.

313
00:19:47,751 --> 00:19:53,745
The log says it loaded the root .env file,
and found the root_agent in baseline.agent.

314
00:19:53,983 --> 00:19:56,946
Two more slips:
the quick start passes a file

315
00:19:56,946 --> 00:19:58,723
where a folder is expected,

316
00:19:58,723 --> 00:20:02,943
and the module READMEs drop the word web,
so it only prints help.

317
00:20:03,183 --> 00:20:07,520
And if llm-rag's Chroma
still holds port 8000, they clash;

318
00:20:07,520 --> 00:20:11,034
the web server stopped:
address already in use.

319
00:20:11,354 --> 00:20:15,855
Run docker compose down first,
or add --port 8080.

320
00:20:16,175 --> 00:20:19,204
Pause, and get the dev UI open on baseline.

321
00:20:22,550 --> 00:20:26,211
Baseline holds three LLM agents,
each with a model,

322
00:20:26,211 --> 00:20:28,651
an instruction, and an output_key.

323
00:20:28,971 --> 00:20:30,541
In the lecture's blocks:

324
00:20:30,541 --> 00:20:33,942
the LLM core, the instructions,
and no tools at all.

325
00:20:34,483 --> 00:20:39,496
A sequential agent runs them in order:
validator, planner, summarizer.

326
00:20:39,816 --> 00:20:42,996
An extra request shows
the tree the UI loads,

327
00:20:42,996 --> 00:20:46,387
with the model from your root .env;
check yours.

328
00:20:46,633 --> 00:20:51,796
Another extra request creates a session:
an empty state, and no events yet.

329
00:20:52,116 --> 00:20:54,420
Think of the state as a shared whiteboard.

330
00:20:54,667 --> 00:20:57,078
Once you add your key, the Runner does this.

331
00:20:57,398 --> 00:21:01,503
The validator's reply goes into the state,
under its output_key;

332
00:21:01,503 --> 00:21:03,043
before the planner runs,

333
00:21:03,043 --> 00:21:06,571
its instruction's placeholder
is filled from that cell.

334
00:21:07,433 --> 00:21:10,228
The summarizer reads the plan the same way.

335
00:21:10,548 --> 00:21:12,563
The agents never call each other:

336
00:21:12,563 --> 00:21:16,777
the session carries the data,
in its state and its history of events.

337
00:21:17,097 --> 00:21:19,700
A parallel agent fills its cells at once.

338
00:21:19,950 --> 00:21:22,574
The lecture's planner needs live systems;

339
00:21:22,574 --> 00:21:26,798
this pipeline has no tools,
so its prices are the model's guesses.

340
00:21:27,118 --> 00:21:31,043
A later notebook gets prices from tools
and sums from Python.

341
00:21:31,583 --> 00:21:33,994
The ADK README calls this memory.

342
00:21:34,314 --> 00:21:38,026
The code calls it session state,
and it lasts one session;

343
00:21:38,026 --> 00:21:40,943
the lecture's memory
remembers you next time,

344
00:21:40,943 --> 00:21:43,018
which none of today's runs does.

345
00:21:43,267 --> 00:21:47,427
Without a real key, a trip request
gets an Internal Server Error.

346
00:21:47,747 --> 00:21:51,011
The server log says why: API key not valid.

347
00:21:51,331 --> 00:21:54,189
The placeholder reached Google,
and was refused.

348
00:21:55,233 --> 00:21:58,203
With your key,
look for three replies in order,

349
00:21:58,203 --> 00:22:03,510
and three new entries in the State tab;
the session is a file in the agent's folder.

350
00:22:03,830 --> 00:22:07,671
Each request is three model calls:
pause, and plan a trip.

351
00:22:11,017 --> 00:22:13,577
The lecture sorts tools into three families.

352
00:22:13,897 --> 00:22:16,785
Data tools fetch, like our book searches;

353
00:22:16,785 --> 00:22:21,786
action tools change something,
like finalizing a trip or sending email;

354
00:22:21,786 --> 00:22:25,097
orchestration tools hand work
to another agent.

355
00:22:25,334 --> 00:22:29,814
Now wire one agent to ten services,
each with its own protocol.

356
00:22:30,134 --> 00:22:32,608
The lecture asks: do you see a problem?

357
00:22:37,850 --> 00:22:40,431
Every tool becomes a custom integration.

358
00:22:40,751 --> 00:22:44,825
MCP, the Model Context Protocol,
makes it one pattern:

359
00:22:44,825 --> 00:22:49,050
a host with clients,
and an MCP server for each service.

360
00:22:49,300 --> 00:22:53,951
The lecture's MCP box is a shared protocol,
not a program you run.

361
00:22:54,271 --> 00:22:58,324
In code, each server is its own plug,
and the tutorial has three.

362
00:22:58,567 --> 00:23:01,916
Plug 1 is in the ADK module on MCP.

363
00:23:02,236 --> 00:23:03,388
Install Node.

364
00:23:06,550 --> 00:23:10,518
The agent's toolset starts
a filesystem server with npx,

365
00:23:10,518 --> 00:23:14,700
as a child process,
talking over standard input and output.

366
00:23:14,950 --> 00:23:19,579
An extra script asks that toolset
for its tools, with no model involved.

367
00:23:19,899 --> 00:23:22,857
The server
offers 14, including write and edit,

368
00:23:22,857 --> 00:23:24,998
and the filter lets three through.

369
00:23:25,318 --> 00:23:27,964
With your key, ask the agent to list files.

370
00:23:30,200 --> 00:23:32,205
Plug 2 is back in the Cheese Quiz.

371
00:23:32,525 --> 00:23:36,189
After a first tool written
as a plain function,

372
00:23:36,189 --> 00:23:42,894
the notebook connects to a remote MCP server
by URL, passed as mcp_servers, not tools.

373
00:23:43,134 --> 00:23:45,566
Extra curl commands talk to that server.

374
00:23:45,886 --> 00:23:48,845
A browser's plain request gets 406:

375
00:23:48,845 --> 00:23:52,905
the server is up,
but speaks MCP, not web pages.

376
00:23:53,225 --> 00:23:57,534
Then initialize, list tools, call:
one tool, one cheese fact per call.

377
00:23:59,284 --> 00:24:00,998
Plug 3 is optional:

378
00:24:00,998 --> 00:24:07,135
the notebook runs its own FastMCP server,
in a thread, on port 8001.

379
00:24:07,455 --> 00:24:13,108
A small slip: the comment above says 8000,
and so does an older saved output.

380
00:24:17,700 --> 00:24:20,751
Notice who does the talking:
your client process.

381
00:24:21,071 --> 00:24:25,892
MCP gives the model no network access;
it still only writes calls.

382
00:24:26,212 --> 00:24:28,431
Pause, and run the MCP cells.

383
00:24:31,767 --> 00:24:35,161
In the lecture,
deep research is a tree of agents:

384
00:24:35,161 --> 00:24:36,790
a lead agent, a planner,

385
00:24:36,790 --> 00:24:40,407
parallel web searchers,
and a synthesis into a report.

386
00:24:40,727 --> 00:24:45,143
The tutorial builds trees like it:
the Cheese Quiz Guild, and the newsletter.

387
00:24:45,384 --> 00:24:48,763
The lecture's planner raises the question
that sorts them:

388
00:24:48,763 --> 00:24:49,928
who plans the order?

389
00:24:50,248 --> 00:24:51,933
First answer: the code.

390
00:24:52,253 --> 00:24:56,776
The sequential agent is an assembly line,
its order fixed in Python.

391
00:24:58,584 --> 00:25:00,333
Second answer: the model.

392
00:25:00,653 --> 00:25:03,691
In the Guild,
each specialist becomes a tool:

393
00:25:03,691 --> 00:25:05,987
a whole agent behind one function.

394
00:25:06,307 --> 00:25:08,781
An orchestrator decides which to call.

395
00:25:09,017 --> 00:25:10,377
Its instructions say:

396
00:25:10,377 --> 00:25:14,201
follow the assembly line strictly,
at most 2 review rounds.

397
00:25:14,521 --> 00:25:19,428
That's a rule in prose; no code enforces it,
except a cap of 12 turns.

398
00:25:19,667 --> 00:25:23,519
The staff's saved run
logged three errors getting a response,

399
00:25:23,519 --> 00:25:27,688
and still produced a quiz;
the log doesn't say which calls failed.

400
00:25:28,008 --> 00:25:32,638
Every hop adds cost, time,
and a way to fail; pause, and run the Guild.

401
00:25:36,784 --> 00:25:39,835
Third answer:
Python, reading the model's label.

402
00:25:40,155 --> 00:25:44,220
In this custom agent,
a classifier writes one word into the state,

403
00:25:44,220 --> 00:25:46,746
and plain if statements pick the handler.

404
00:25:53,584 --> 00:25:55,653
The else branch: clarification.

405
00:25:55,973 --> 00:25:57,618
The comparison is exact;

406
00:25:57,618 --> 00:26:01,456
lowercase the label first,
or ask for structured output.

407
00:26:01,776 --> 00:26:03,546
The exercises try both.

408
00:26:05,284 --> 00:26:09,017
The lecture also lists handoffs
among the orchestration tools.

409
00:26:09,337 --> 00:26:13,988
These tutorials use agents as tools;
lecture 7 sketches handoffs.

410
00:26:15,033 --> 00:26:18,212
The Travel Planner notebook
rehearses the lecture's trip.

411
00:26:18,532 --> 00:26:22,350
Copy it, add your key,
and install its pinned agents package.

412
00:26:22,670 --> 00:26:26,606
Paris shrinks to a mock catalog:
3 cities, 6 hotels,

413
00:26:26,606 --> 00:26:30,542
5 search tools and a finalize tool,
booking nothing.

414
00:26:30,783 --> 00:26:34,687
In the staff's saved run,
turn 1 asks which California city.

415
00:26:35,007 --> 00:26:38,122
Turn 2 runs five searches
and offers options.

416
00:26:38,442 --> 00:26:41,944
In turn 3 the user picks,
the model calls finalize,

417
00:26:41,944 --> 00:26:45,652
and the reply shows the exact plan
and asks for a yes.

418
00:26:45,900 --> 00:26:46,988
That's the gate.

419
00:26:47,308 --> 00:26:50,648
A finalize call from the model
can only propose:

420
00:26:50,648 --> 00:26:53,084
if the plan isn't the approved one,

421
00:26:53,084 --> 00:26:57,398
the code stores it as pending,
and returns needs_confirmation.

422
00:26:57,633 --> 00:27:01,625
Approval lives in a separate function,
apply_confirmation:

423
00:27:01,625 --> 00:27:04,929
only an explicit yes
copies pending to approved.

424
00:27:14,366 --> 00:27:17,842
Because apply_confirmation runs
on the human's message,

425
00:27:17,842 --> 00:27:19,358
before the agent starts.

426
00:27:19,678 --> 00:27:24,585
Inside turn 3, nothing is approved,
so every retry gets the same stamp.

427
00:27:24,833 --> 00:27:26,966
Turn 4: the human types yes.

428
00:27:27,286 --> 00:27:31,005
The stamp flips to approved, finalize runs,

429
00:27:31,005 --> 00:27:37,057
and Python builds the budget line by line:
2,690 of the 6,000 dollars.

430
00:27:37,377 --> 00:27:40,214
The notebook's own test checks
Python's number.

431
00:27:40,450 --> 00:27:44,098
What the user sees comes from checked state,
not the model's prose.

432
00:27:44,418 --> 00:27:48,018
An itinerary
that skipped the check raises an error,

433
00:27:48,018 --> 00:27:49,195
a hard guardrail;

434
00:27:49,195 --> 00:27:54,039
a dollar amount in the model's prose
only prints a warning, a soft one.

435
00:27:54,283 --> 00:27:57,625
The lecture puts success criteria
in the instructions,

436
00:27:57,625 --> 00:27:59,915
and guardrails in the decision maker.

437
00:28:00,235 --> 00:28:05,504
This notebook keeps both in code the model
can't talk its way past, on the action tool.

438
00:28:05,824 --> 00:28:09,024
Every turn is paid;
pause, and run the four turns.

439
00:28:12,067 --> 00:28:15,392
The last notebook writes
a cheese newsletter per reader,

440
00:28:15,392 --> 00:28:17,827
with Microsoft's Agent Framework, pinned.

441
00:28:18,147 --> 00:28:22,030
In the USERS cell, put in yourself
and a friend, and keep two entries.

442
00:28:22,350 --> 00:28:25,080
Email is preview only; nothing is sent.

443
00:28:25,317 --> 00:28:27,476
The orchestrator gets three tools:

444
00:28:27,476 --> 00:28:31,034
a researcher with web search,
an editor, and send_email.

445
00:28:31,354 --> 00:28:34,725
At most 16 iterations,
one tool call at a time.

446
00:28:34,967 --> 00:28:39,082
In the staff's saved run,
the first city took four editor calls;

447
00:28:39,082 --> 00:28:41,036
the code validates every draft

448
00:28:41,036 --> 00:28:44,098
and hands any errors back
as the tool's result.

449
00:28:44,418 --> 00:28:46,743
Then send_email saved the HTML.

450
00:28:47,063 --> 00:28:50,199
That run had four readers;
today's list makes two.

451
00:28:50,434 --> 00:28:51,586
Now the harness.

452
00:28:51,906 --> 00:28:53,730
When the orchestrator stops,

453
00:28:53,730 --> 00:28:56,794
Python ignores its words
and checks two things:

454
00:28:56,794 --> 00:29:00,717
is there a receipt from send_email,
and is that file on disk?

455
00:29:01,037 --> 00:29:04,962
If not, the run fails;
success is a receipt, not a claim.

456
00:29:05,200 --> 00:29:07,739
Compare a rule
that lives only in the prompt.

457
00:29:08,059 --> 00:29:11,067
The editor is told: no embedded URLs.

458
00:29:11,387 --> 00:29:15,546
The saved preview has one anyway,
because no code checks for it;

459
00:29:15,546 --> 00:29:16,976
a note is not a check.

460
00:29:17,217 --> 00:29:22,540
The lecture's Self-RAG and Corrective RAG
grade their retrieval for the same reason:

461
00:29:22,540 --> 00:29:23,745
don't trust, check.

462
00:29:24,065 --> 00:29:26,689
Next lecture calls
this outer layer the harness.

463
00:29:27,009 --> 00:29:30,401
Web search is billed per call;
pause, and run the demo.

464
00:29:33,450 --> 00:29:36,266
Tear down: Ctrl+C stops the web server.

465
00:29:36,586 --> 00:29:39,704
An extra git status
shows the renamed folders

466
00:29:39,704 --> 00:29:41,089
and your Python pin;

467
00:29:41,089 --> 00:29:45,183
.env, the virtual environment
and the lock file are ignored.

468
00:29:45,417 --> 00:29:49,352
The sessions in the agent's .adk/
folder aren't ignored,

469
00:29:49,352 --> 00:29:50,828
so don't commit them,

470
00:29:50,828 --> 00:29:55,337
and delete the 1.6 gigabyte
virtual environment when you're done.

471
00:29:55,583 --> 00:29:58,698
In each notebook,
disconnect and delete the runtime.

472
00:29:59,018 --> 00:30:03,018
Keep the service account key beside llm-rag,
never inside,

473
00:30:03,018 --> 00:30:07,018
and delete or cap the other two keys
when the course ends.

474
00:30:08,267 --> 00:30:11,446
Here's the lecture's anatomy,
filled with today's code.

475
00:30:11,766 --> 00:30:18,212
Gemini and OpenAI models as the core;
prompts and output types as instructions;

476
00:30:18,212 --> 00:30:24,331
tools by hand, decorated, or over MCP;
history and session state as memory.

477
00:30:24,567 --> 00:30:28,578
Dynamic control is the framework's loop,
or two calls by hand.

478
00:30:28,898 --> 00:30:30,465
The lecture's formula,

479
00:30:30,465 --> 00:30:34,811
LLM plus reasoning, instructions and tools,
gains two pieces:

480
00:30:34,811 --> 00:30:39,457
a loop the framework runs,
and checks that your own code enforces.

481
00:30:39,700 --> 00:30:40,809
Test yourself.

482
00:30:41,129 --> 00:30:44,713
One: who runs the function,
and what goes back in call 2?

483
00:30:45,033 --> 00:30:48,660
Two: what does mode="any" change,
and why only on call 1?

484
00:30:48,900 --> 00:30:50,852
Three: what ends an agent loop,

485
00:30:50,852 --> 00:30:54,127
what cap do frameworks add,
and which block is that?

486
00:30:54,447 --> 00:30:57,775
Four: how does the summarizer
get the plan without a message?

487
00:30:58,017 --> 00:31:01,009
Five:
what decides that a harness run succeeded,

488
00:31:01,009 --> 00:31:02,817
and why not the model's word?

489
00:31:03,137 --> 00:31:07,446
Answers and every command
are in the exercises and the study guide.

490
00:31:07,766 --> 00:31:10,027
Next lecture: the harness itself.

